            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "For some reason it only worked for me in the online tool after wrapping in\n\n```\n---- MODULE scratch ----\n...\n====\n```\n\notherwise i would get a parse error. Is it required to have a named module or is it some quirk of the tool?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "This TLA+ spec could be useful to have in a separate file for further extension down the line, and to be able to run the TLA+ specific tools on it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "So I did consider whether I should add it to the repo itself. I had decided against it for the following reasons:\n\n1. I wasn't sure if anyone beside myself would be interested in it\n2. Writing a spec can be quite a personal learning journey\n3. Maintaining the spec over time can be tricky as they really require people to \"live\" them\n\nAs a consequence I consider it a bit temporary in nature - something I used to guide me in the implementation. With respect to that, it really did act like a super-power and prevented me from agonizing with myself over how to represent all the related state - it boiled down to a few choice early-exit conditions, rather than trying to maintain a swamp of switch statements, for instance.\n\nRegarding 2, it tends to be that no-one sees the battles you fight with TLC, as by the time the spec is presented to others, it's generally upholding the desired properties \ud83d\ude0a The battles with TLC _are_ the learning experience and I haven't found an easy way to document them.\n\nThis article captures the essence of my feelings:\n\nhttps://ahelwer.ca/post/2025-07-04-tla-contracts/"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Ah, yeah, that's a silly trap I guess. I'll add them in to ease use; I removed them because I thought they were noisy."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "What if you chose something simpler, like a graphviz (dot file) or mermaid diagram?\n\nThat would be easier without so much of a learning curve.\n\nMade a simple dot file for example purpose:\n\n```\ndigraph \"parsing\" {\n\n\tInit [Label=\"Init\"]\n\tHeader [Label=\"Header\"]\n\tFirmwareDevices [Label=\"FirmwareDevices\"]\n\tDownstreamDevices [Label=\"DownstreamDevices\"]\n\tComponentImageInfo [Label=\"ComponentImageInfos\"]\n\tComplete [Label=\"Complete\"]\n\tUnsupported [Label=\"Unsupported\"]\n\tError [Label=\"Error\"]\n\n\tInit -> Header [label=\"format <= pin\"]\n\tInit -> Unsupported [label=\"format > pin\"]\n\n\tHeader -> FirmwareDevices\n\tHeader -> Error\n\n\tFirmwareDevices -> DownstreamDevices [label=\"pin > 1\"]\n\tFirmwareDevices -> Error\n\n\tDownstreamDevices -> ComponentImageInfo [label=\"pin > 1\"]\n\tDownstreamDevices -> Error\n\n\tFirmwareDevices -> ComponentImageInfo [label=\"pin == 1\"]\n\n\tComponentImageInfo -> Complete\n\tComponentImageInfo -> Error\n}\n```\n\ncan use \n\n`dot -Tpng pldm_pkg.dot > pldm_pkg.png`  to render.\n\nThen contributors could render that locally or refer to what's in the repo (github renders mermaid, not sure about dot) and refer to it.\n\n\nThen we already have the precondition state for the function and the postcondition documented."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for putting in the effort to translate the spec into dot, it was nice to render it and take a look.\n\nSo I did consider mermaid initially, but then decided that if I was going to put the effort in to specifying a state machine, I'd much rather do so in a way that was testable for the properties I needed, hence TLA+.\n\nWe should keep in mind what we're trying to achieve here. The spec and the state machine cover _implementation details_ of the library - a means to catch erroneous use of the APIs, given the problems with enforcing sequencing via the type system. The user-facing result of the state machine is the errors that are visible to the callers of the APIs. We should make sure *those* are documented appropriately, with descriptions of the circumstances in which specific error values arise, and the pre- and post- conditions as you suggested.\n\nOtherwise, the information on the sequencing required can be gleaned from DSP0267 in the context of the desired format revision to pin against. I don't think we should cater to people who aren't willing to read the spec that the APIs implement.\n\nI rather we be conservative about including implementer-oriented documentation because the implementation can change on a whim, and all of a sudden the documentation is incorrect. I'm trying to avoid increasing my maintenance burden by acknowledging that some information is only temporally relevant, and locating it in time with the change (by putting it in the commit message)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 22,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 100,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Could use a `Tested` statement with the commands/tools you used to check this spec. \n\nCould check this for example `https://will62794.github.io/spectacle/` or with local tools."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 100,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm not sure there's much value in that; if you've come far enough to understand what is going on above, no doubt you have access to the tools needed to exercise the spec with a model checker. I mentioned above setting `NR_FORMATS <- 4`. I think that's enough to reproduce?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 100,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 101,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Thank you!"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 101,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "It makes me happy that you share some enthusiasm for this kind of thing \ud83d\ude0a"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Did not look into the C code much so far."
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/libpldm/firmware_update.h",
                    "line": 2284,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "`Unsupported` and `Error` states are missing here, should we add those to match the TLA+ spec?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/libpldm/firmware_update.h",
                    "line": 2284,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "In my opinion, no; `Unsupported` and `Error` are represented in the implementation in return values and incorrect state for the current action."
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/libpldm/firmware_update.h",
                    "line": 2284,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Then the implementation does not match the state machine, right?\n\nI thought it should match. From the code perspective, i agree, the implemented behavior still works as the precondition state will be wrong in error case.\n\nBut from contributor perspective it might be harder to reason about since the state machine is not making progress and not ending up in one of the 3 final states."
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/libpldm/firmware_update.h",
                    "line": 2284,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Then the implementation does not match the state machine, right?\n\nTo give myself some credit here, I never claimed that it did. The commit message says:\n\n> The state machine implementation is **modeled** on the TLA+ specification below\n\n(emphasis added)\n\nThe intent was that \"modeled\" suggested relative alignment rather than strict alignment between the two.\n\nFurther, there are details the TLA+ specification omits - it only tackles one specific problem, so you can claim it's not accurate regardless of whether we fix this issue.\n\nThat said, the spec was about setting expectations for the implementation, and I think you make a reasonable point that it diverges without any real discussion. I will update the commit message to describe how the `Unsupported` and `Error` states map to the implementation, which will hopefully reduce the surprise.\n\n> since the state machine is not making progress and not ending up in one of the 3 final states\n\nThe reason that it doesn't is that the APIs shouldn't observe the problem in practice, because the callers are expected to perform appropriate error handling. This will detect the incorrect use and cause progress to stop (by way of no further calls into the APIs). Incorrect use should be caught quickly in testing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "include/libpldm/firmware_update.h",
                    "line": 2284,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "ok, i see there is some tradeoff as fully conforming to the state machine would introduce extra code and runtime overhead which is redundant if the caller handles error codes."
                }
            ],
